			<article>
				<p class="eyebrow">Tutorial</p>
				<h1>Run your first agent in Zoes</h1>
				<p class="lede">In this tutorial, we install Zoes on a Mac, start Claude Code on one of your git repositories, watch it work, and review and commit the change it makes.</p>
				<p>You need a Mac with macOS 13 or later and a git repository on it. On Linux or Windows, follow <a href="/docs/install">Install Zoes</a> for the first step, then continue here.</p>

				<h2 id="install-anywhere">Install Zoes</h2>
				<ol>
					<li>Download <a href="https://github.com/mingo023/anywhere/releases/latest/download/Zoes.dmg">Zoes.dmg</a>.</li>
					<li>Open the file and drag <strong>Zoes</strong> to <strong>Applications</strong>.</li>
				</ol>
				<p>Don't open Zoes yet. We install an agent first, so Zoes finds it on first launch.</p>

				<h2 id="install-claude-code-and-sign-in">Install Claude Code and sign in</h2>
				<p>Zoes runs the agent command line tools that it finds on your Mac. We use Claude Code in this tutorial. If Claude Code is already installed and signed in, open Zoes and go to the next step.</p>
				<ol>
					<li>Install Claude Code with the instructions on <a href="https://claude.com/product/claude-code">the Claude Code page</a>.</li>
					<li>In Terminal, sign in:
				<pre><code>claude auth login</code></pre>
					</li>
					<li>Open Zoes from <strong>Applications</strong>.</li>
				</ol>
				<p>The Zoes window opens with the sidebar on the left. On first launch, the app registers its background service, pocketd, as a login item. pocketd runs the agents, so they keep working after you close the window.</p>
				<p>To confirm that Zoes found Claude Code, open Settings with <kbd>⌘,</kbd> and select <strong>Agents</strong>. Claude Code is in the <strong>Providers</strong> list. If you see <code>Claude Code not found</code> instead, check that <code>claude</code> runs in a new Terminal window, then restart Zoes.</p>
				<p>To use another agent, see <a href="/docs/agents">Supported agents</a>.</p>

				<h2 id="add-a-project">Add a project</h2>
				<ol>
					<li>In the sidebar, click the folder button at the top of the projects list.</li>
					<li>Click <strong>Add project…</strong>.</li>
					<li>Select the folder of your git repository and confirm.</li>
				</ol>
				<p>The project now shows in the sidebar.</p>

				<h2 id="start-a-session">Start a session</h2>
				<ol>
					<li>Press <kbd>⌘N</kbd>. The new session page opens and asks <strong>What should we build next?</strong></li>
					<li>Check that the project chip shows your project and that the agent is Claude Code.</li>
					<li>Type a small, safe request. For example:
				<pre><code>Add a short section to README.md that explains how to run the tests.</code></pre>
					</li>
					<li>Press <kbd>Return</kbd>.</li>
				</ol>
				<p>The session opens as a chat, and Claude Code starts on the request. It works in your project's own checkout, on your current branch. To give an agent its own branch instead, see <a href="/docs/worktrees">Start an agent in a new worktree</a>.</p>

				<h2 id="watch-its-status">Watch its status</h2>
				<p>The session's row in the sidebar shows its status. While Claude Code runs a turn, the row shows a spinner. That status is <span class="status working">Working</span>.</p>
				<p>If Claude Code asks for permission, for example to run a command, the status changes to <span class="status needs-you">Needs you</span>. The row shows an amber dot, and the session goes into the Inbox. Answer in the chat to let it continue.</p>
				<p>Now switch to another app and wait for the turn to end. When it ends, Zoes shows a banner, and the row shows a green dot and its title in bold. That status is <span class="status done">Done</span>. It means that a turn ended and nobody has seen it yet. If you watch the chat while the turn ends, the session goes straight to Idle instead.</p>
				<p>Click the bell at the top of the sidebar to open the <strong>Inbox</strong>. It lists the sessions that need you, and the Done and Failed ones you haven't seen. For every status and where it shows, see <a href="/docs/sessions">Sessions and status</a>.</p>

				<h2 id="review-its-changes">Review its changes</h2>
				<ol>
					<li>On the right edge of the window, click the branch icon to open <strong>Changes</strong>. The icon shows the number of changed files.</li>
					<li>Under <strong>Changes</strong>, click <code>README.md</code>. Its diff opens.</li>
					<li>Read the diff. If you want a different result, ask Claude Code for changes in the chat.</li>
					<li>In the commit box, type a message, such as <code>Explain how to run the tests</code>.</li>
					<li>Click <strong>Commit All</strong>, or press <kbd>⌘↩</kbd>. With nothing staged, Zoes commits every changed file.</li>
				</ol>
				<p>The file leaves the list, and the panel shows <strong>No changes.</strong> You have run an agent, followed its status, and committed its work.</p>

				<h2 id="next-steps">Next steps</h2>
				<ul>
					<li><a href="/docs/worktrees">Start an agent in a new worktree</a>, so several agents work at once without touching your branch.</li>
					<li><a href="/docs/review">Review and commit an agent's changes</a>, including pull requests.</li>
					<li><a href="/docs/install#install-the-anywhere-command">Install the <code>zoes</code> command</a> to start sessions from scripts.</li>
					<li><a href="/docs/architecture">About how Zoes runs on your machines</a>.</li>
				</ul>
				<p>Longer guides are on the blog: <a href="/blog/run-multiple-claude-code-agents-in-parallel">run several Claude Code agents in parallel</a>, <a href="/blog/run-multiple-codex-agents-git-worktrees">run several Codex agents in git worktrees</a>, and <a href="/blog/script-claude-code-codex-from-cli">script agents from your shell</a>.</p>
